Genomic studies of Nigerian and African E. coli, and how public genome data have been used.
Genomic work on African E. coli has grown. It now includes multi-country and One Health surveillance. Even so, African genomes remain under-represented in global collections compared with the continent's burden of infectious disease (Lewis et al., 2023; Musicha et al., 2017). A systematic review of diarrhoeagenic E. coli (DEC) in Africa shows how narrow the evidence is. Of the underlying studies, 80% were hospital-based, 91% focused on children, and only 4.4% described outbreaks (Kalule et al., 2024).
4.1 Nigeria: clinical and carriage isolates
Most Nigerian genomic work comes from the south-west, especially Ibadan. In a study of 477 children under five in northern Ibadan, DEC carriage was common and the children's well water was contaminated (Akinlabi et al., 2023) and isolates from related case–control studies were later sequenced. Akinlabi et al. characterised 96 enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) isolates for virulence genes, serotypes and phylogeny. EPEC had rarely been typed molecularly in Nigeria before this (Akinlabi et al., 2025).
Among hospital isolates, Medugu et al. sequenced 107 multidrug-resistant E. coli from a tertiary hospital (2019–2020). Virulence genes were common, 81.3% of them were linked to mobile genetic elements, and virulence and resistance genes sat together on plasmids (Medugu et al., 2025). In the same isolates, fluoroquinolone resistance came mostly from chromosomal gyrA and parC mutations. Plasmid-mediated resistance contributed little (Medugu et al., 2026). A 2026 preprint extends this work to Ekiti and Ondo States and looks for signs of zoonotic, human-to-human and environmental transmission (Uzairue et al., 2026).
4.2 Nigeria: food, animal and environmental sources
A second group of studies follows E. coli along the food chain. Aworh et al. examined ESBL-producing E. coli in abattoir workers, beef cattle and abattoir environments in Abuja and Lagos. Their findings were consistent with transmission from animals to people along the beef chain (Aworh et al., 2022). In Kwara State, sequencing of ESBL-producing isolates from poultry showed multidrug resistance together with mobile elements, virulence genes and phages (Al-Mustapha et al., 2023). Fish have also been sampled. Isolates from catfish caught in the River Niger in Anambra State were characterised by MLST and cgMLST (Ekwuazi et al., 2024). A single poultry-associated isolate from Enugu State carried resistance and virulence genes on both the chromosome and plasmids (Ejikeugwu et al., 2026).

